<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-trigonometric-functions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Trigonometric Functions</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.1 Functions
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Determine the inverse of a function of polynomials and trigonometric ratios
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on activity -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    15 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To let learners use the unit circle to notice the patterns in sine, cosine, and tangent values before the formal definitions are introduced.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Guide learners through the unit circle diagram first, making sure they can read the coordinates at each quadrantal angle. Then let them complete the table in pairs. Circulate and ask why tangent becomes undefined at certain angles, rather than telling them directly.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="activity-exploring-core-trig-functions">
        <p>
            Consider the unit circle centered at the origin with radius 1. For an angle <m>x</m>, the coordinates of the point on the circle are given by <m>(\cos(x), \sin(x))</m>. The tangent function is defined as the ratio
            <m>\tan(x)=\frac{\sin(x)}{\cos(x)}</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Complete the table below by evaluating the sine, cosine, and tangent values for each quadrantal angle.
        </p>

        <table>
            <tabular top="major" bottom="major" left="medium" right="medium">
                <row>
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\pi</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\frac{3\pi}{2}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>2\pi</m></cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell><m>\sin(x)</m></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell><m>\cos(x)</m></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell><m>\tan(x)</m></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            Use your completed table and the unit circle diagram to answer the following questions.
        </p>

        <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Which angles make the tangent function undefined? Explain your observation using the formula <m>\tan(x)=\frac{\sin(x)}{\cos(x)}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Describe how the sine and cosine values change as the angle moves around the unit circle.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Identify the maximum and minimum values of the sine and cosine functions.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Compare the periods of the sine, cosine, and tangent functions.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Predict the values of <m>\sin(3\pi)</m> and <m>\cos(3\pi)</m> using the periodic behavior of the functions.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-unit-circle-activity">
            <caption>Key quadrantal angles on the unit circle</caption>

            <image width="55%">
                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2.2]

                        \draw (0,0) circle (1);

                        \draw[-&gt;] (-1.3,0)--(1.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
                        \draw[-&gt;] (0,-1.3)--(0,1.3) node[above] {$y$};

                        \filldraw[blue] (1,0) circle (1pt) node[below right] {$\left(1,0\right)$};
                        \filldraw[blue] (0,1) circle (1pt) node[above left] {$\left(0,1\right)$};
                        \filldraw[blue] (-1,0) circle (1pt) node[below left] {$\left(-1,0\right)$};
                        \filldraw[blue] (0,-1) circle (1pt) node[below left] {$\left(0,-1\right)$};

                        \node at (0.3,0.15) {$0$};
                        \node at (0.2,1.15) {$\frac{\pi}{2}$};
                        \node at (-1.15,0.15) {$\pi$};
                        \node at (-0.25,-1.15) {$\frac{3\pi}{2}$};

                    \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
    </activity>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on structured lesson plan-->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    20 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalize the unit circle definitions of sine, cosine, and tangent, and to connect them to the quadrant sign patterns learners will use throughout the topic.
                </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways and Teacher Connection</title>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> Refer back to the values learners found in the activity table. Use these to introduce the formal definitions, where cosine is the horizontal coordinate and sine is the vertical coordinate of the point on the unit circle.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Domain, Range, and Period:</alert> When presenting the domain and range, use inequality notation rather than brackets. For example, write the range of sine as <m>-1 \le y \le 1</m> rather than using square brackets, since this is easier for learners to read.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Quadrant Signs:</alert> Introduce the mnemonic for remembering which functions are positive in each quadrant. Ask learners to test it themselves using values from their activity table before moving to the practice examples.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>


    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

        <insight>
        <p>
            For any angle <m>x</m> measured from the positive <m>x</m>-axis, the point where the terminal side of the angle meets the unit circle has coordinates <m>(\cos(x), \sin(x))</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            This means the coordinates of a point on the unit circle define the trigonometric functions. The horizontal coordinate gives the cosine of the angle, and the vertical coordinate gives the sine of the angle.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-trig-definitions">
            <caption>Unit circle definitions of trigonometric functions</caption>
            <image width="50%" xml:id="img-trig-definitions">
                <shortdescription>
                    A unit circle on a coordinate grid showing an angle in standard position in Quadrant II. 
                    The terminal side intersects the circle at point (cos(x), sin(x)). A dashed vertical line 
                    to the negative x-axis is labeled cosine of x, and a dashed horizontal line to the 
                    positive y-axis is labeled sine of x.
                </shortdescription>
                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2.5, &gt;=stealth]
                        % Draw Axes
                        \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (-1.3, 0) -- (1.3, 0) node[right] {$x$};
                        \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (0, -1.3) -- (0, 1.3) node[above] {$y$};

                        % Draw Unit Circle
                        \draw[gray!50, thin] (0,0) circle (1);

                        % Angle 'x' (theta) in standard position (Quadrant II)
                        \def\theta{135}
                        \coordinate (O) at (0,0);
                        \coordinate (P) at ({\theta}:1);
                        
                        % Terminal side and origin labeling
                        \draw[thick, blue] (O) -- (P);
                        \filldraw[black] (O) circle (0.5pt) node[below left, font=\scriptsize] {$O$};

                        % Coordinates of point P - labels are made prominent and slightly shifted
                        \draw[dashed, blue] ({cos(\theta)},0) -- (P) node[midway, right, xshift=2pt, blue!90!black] {$\sin(x)$};
                        \draw[dashed, blue] (0,0) -- ({cos(\theta)},0) node[midway, below, red!90!black] {$\cos(x)$};
                        \filldraw (P) circle (1.2pt) node[above left] {$(\cos(x), \sin(x))$};

                        % Angle arc labeling
                        \draw[-&gt;, blue] (0.25,0) arc (0:\theta:0.25);
                        \node[blue, font=\small] at (0.4, 0.4) {$x$};
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
            From the unit circle, we can describe the three main trigonometric functions as follows.
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The <alert>cosine</alert> of an angle is the horizontal coordinate of the corresponding point on the unit circle.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        <md>
                            <mrow>\cos(x)=x\text{-coordinate}</mrow>
                        </md>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Domain is all real numbers, written as <m>-\infty &lt; x &lt; \infty</m>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Range is <m>-1 \le y \le 1</m>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Period is <m>2\pi</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        The <alert>sine</alert> of an angle is the vertical coordinate of the corresponding point on the unit circle.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        <md>
                            <mrow>\sin(x)=y\text{-coordinate}</mrow>
                        </md>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Domain is all real numbers, written as <m>-\infty &lt; x &lt; \infty</m>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Range is <m>-1 \le y \le 1</m>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Period is <m>2\pi</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        The <alert>tangent</alert> of an angle is found by dividing the sine of the angle by its cosine.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        <md>
                            <mrow>\tan(x)=\frac{\sin(x)}{\cos(x)}</mrow>
                        </md>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Since division by zero is undefined, the tangent function has no value whenever <m>\cos(x)=0</m>. This happens at odd multiples of <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Domain is all real numbers except odd multiples of <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Range is all real numbers, written as <m>-\infty &lt; y &lt; \infty</m>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Period is <m>\pi</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
            The sign of each trigonometric function depends on the quadrant where the terminal side of the angle lies. This can be read directly from the signs of the coordinates on the unit circle.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-trig-signs-quadrants">
            <caption>Algebraic signs of trigonometric functions by quadrant</caption>
            <image width="60%" xml:id="img-trig-signs-quadrants">
                <shortdescription>
                    A diagram of a coordinate plane showing the four quadrants (I, II, III, IV).
                    The labels within each quadrant indicate which trigonometric functions (Sine, Cosine, Tangent) are positive. 
                    Quadrant I: All are positive. 
                    Quadrant II: Only Sine is positive. 
                    Quadrant III: Only Tangent is positive. 
                    Quadrant IV: Only Cosine is positive.
                </shortdescription>
                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2.5, &gt;=stealth]
                        % Draw Axes
                        \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (-1.3, 0) -- (1.3, 0) node[right] {$x$};
                        \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (0, -1.3) -- (0, 1.3) node[above] {$y$};

                        % Draw Unit Circle
                        \draw[gray!50, thin] (0,0) circle (1);

                        % Define a distance from the origin for labels to ensure they are inside circle
                        \def\r{0.65}

                        % --- Quadrant Labels ---
                        \node[font=\large\bfseries] at (0.9, 0.9) {I};
                        \node[font=\large\bfseries] at (-0.9, 0.9) {II};
                        \node[font=\large\bfseries] at (-0.9, -0.9) {III};
                        \node[font=\large\bfseries] at (0.9, -0.9) {IV};

                        % --- Trigonometric Signs Mnemonic ---
                        
                        % Quadrant I: All Positive
                        \node[align=center, text=blue!80!black] at ({45}:\r) {
                            \textbf{All Pos.}\\
                            $\sin \theta &gt; 0$\\
                            $\cos \theta &gt; 0$\\
                            $\tan \theta &gt; 0$
                        };

                        % Quadrant II: Sine Positive
                        \node[align=center, text=green!60!black] at ({135}:\r) {
                            \textbf{Sine Pos.}\\
                            $\sin \theta &gt; 0$
                        };

                        % Quadrant III: Tangent Positive
                        \node[align=center, text=orange!80!black] at ({225}:\r) {
                            \textbf{Tangent Pos.}\\
                            $\tan \theta &gt; 0$
                        };

                        % Quadrant IV: Cosine Positive
                        \node[align=center, text=red!80!black] at ({315}:\r) {
                            \textbf{Cosine Pos.}\\
                            $\cos \theta &gt; 0$
                        };

                    \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
            The diagram shows which trigonometric functions are positive in each quadrant. A popular mnemonic used to remember this counterclockwise sequence from <term>Quadrant I</term> to <term>Quadrant IV</term> is <q>All Students Take Chemistry</q> or <q>Add Sugar To Coffee.</q>
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        In <alert>Quadrant I</alert>, all three functions are positive.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        In <alert>Quadrant II</alert>, only sine is positive.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        In <alert>Quadrant III</alert>, only tangent is positive.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        In <alert>Quadrant IV</alert>, only cosine is positive.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
            Remembering these sign patterns helps when evaluating trigonometric functions for angles greater than <m>90^\circ</m> or when finding the sign of a trigonometric ratio without a calculator.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->

    <example xml:id="ex-eval-sine-unit-circle">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Determine the exact value of <m>\sin\left(\frac{5\pi}{6}\right)</m> using the unit circle.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                The angle <m>\frac{5\pi}{6}</m> is located in Quadrant II. To evaluate the sine function, we first determine the reference angle by subtracting our angle from <m>\pi</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>\pi - \frac{5\pi}{6} = \frac{\pi}{6}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                In Quadrant II, the sine function yields positive values. We already know the sine of this reference angle.
            </p>

            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right) = \frac{1}{2}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                We can conclude that the exact value matches the reference sine.
            </p>

            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sin\left(\frac{5\pi}{6}\right) = \frac{1}{2}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-sine-five-pi-six-improved">
                <caption>Sine value in Quadrant II</caption>
                <image width="45%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2]
                            \draw (0,0) circle (1);

                            \draw[-&gt;] (-1.2,0)--(1.2,0);
                            \draw[-&gt;] (0,-1.2)--(0,1.2);

                            \coordinate (P) at ({cos(150)},{sin(150)});

                            \draw[thick,blue] (0,0)--(P);
                            \draw[dashed] ({cos(150)},0)--(P);

                            \filldraw[blue] (P) circle (1pt);

                            \node[above left] at (P)
                            {$\left(-\frac{\sqrt3}{2},\frac12\right)$};

                            \draw[-&gt;] (0.3,0) arc (0:150:0.3);
                            \node at (-0.2,0.35) {$\frac{5\pi}{6}$};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-eval-cosine-unit-circle">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Determine the exact value of <m>\cos\left(\frac{4\pi}{3}\right)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                The angle <m>\frac{4\pi}{3}</m> radians terminates in Quadrant III. We find the reference angle by calculating the difference between the given angle and <m>\pi</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>\frac{4\pi}{3} - \pi = \frac{\pi}{3}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Evaluating the cosine of this reference angle yields a familiar positive fraction.
            </p>

            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{3}\right) = \frac{1}{2}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Because <m>x</m>-coordinates (and thus cosine values) are negative in Quadrant III, we apply a negative sign to our result to get the final answer.
            </p>

            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>\cos\left(\frac{4\pi}{3}\right) = -\frac{1}{2}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-cos-four-pi-three-improved">
                <caption>Cosine value in Quadrant III</caption>
                <image width="45%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2]
                            \draw (0,0) circle (1);

                            \draw[-&gt;] (-1.2,0)--(1.2,0);
                            \draw[-&gt;] (0,-1.2)--(0,1.2);

                            \coordinate (P) at ({cos(240)},{sin(240)});

                            \draw[thick,red] (0,0)--(P);
                            \draw[dashed] (0,{sin(240)})--(P);

                            \filldraw[red] (P) circle (1pt);

                            \node[below left] at (P)
                            {$\left(-\frac12,-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right)$};

                            \draw[-&gt;] (0.25,0) arc (0:240:0.25);
                            \node at (-0.25,0.25) {$\frac{4\pi}{3}$};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </example>


    <example xml:id="ex-eval-tangent-unit-circle">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Determine the exact value of <m>\tan\left(-\frac{\pi}{4}\right)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                A negative angle indicates a clockwise rotation from the positive <m>x</m>-axis, placing <m>-\frac{\pi}{4}</m> in Quadrant IV. The corresponding coordinate point on the unit circle is established as follows.
            </p>

            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, -\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                The tangent of an angle is defined as the ratio of its sine (<m>y</m>-coordinate) to its cosine (<m>x</m>-coordinate).
            </p>

            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>\tan(x) = \frac{\sin(x)}{\cos(x)}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                We substitute the coordinates from our unit circle point into this ratio.
            </p>

            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>\tan\left(-\frac{\pi}{4}\right) \amp= \frac{-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}}{\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= -1</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                This simplifies to our exact value.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>\tan\left(-\frac{\pi}{4}\right) = -1</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tan-negative-pi-four-improved">
                <caption>Tangent value in Quadrant IV</caption>
                <image width="45%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2]
                            \draw (0,0) circle (1);

                            \draw[-&gt;] (-1.2,0)--(1.2,0);
                            \draw[-&gt;] (0,-1.2)--(0,1.2);

                            \coordinate (P) at ({cos(-45)},{sin(-45)});

                            \draw[thick,teal] (0,0)--(P);
                            \draw[dashed] ({cos(-45)},0)--(P);

                            \filldraw[teal] (P) circle (1pt);

                            \node[below right] at (P)
                            {$\left(\frac{\sqrt2}{2},-\frac{\sqrt2}{2}\right)$};

                            \draw[-&gt;] (0.35,0) arc (0:-45:0.35);
                            \node at (0.45,-0.2) {$-\frac{\pi}{4}$};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- If STACK questions are included, uncomment this: -->

    <!-- <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" /> -->

    <!-- Add STACK exercises if made -->

    <!-- Add static exercises -->

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-sin-cos-tan">

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate the exact value of <m>\cos(\pi)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>-1</m>
                </p>
            </answer>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The angle <m>\pi</m> radians corresponds to halfway around the unit circle, landing on the negative <m>x</m>-axis. The coordinates here are <m>(-1, 0)</m>. Since cosine is the <m>x</m>-coordinate, the output is <m>-1</m>.
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="fig-ex-cos-pi">
                    <caption>Cosine at <m>\pi</m></caption>
                    <image width="40%">
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5]
                                \draw (0,0) circle (1);
                                \draw[-&gt;] (-1.5,0)--(1.5,0);
                                \draw[-&gt;] (0,-1.5)--(0,1.5);
                                \filldraw[red] (-1,0) circle (1.5pt) node[above left] {$(-1, 0)$};
                                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0.3,0) arc (0:180:0.3) node[midway, above] {$\pi$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Graph one full period of the function <m>y = \sin(x)</m> on the interval <m>[0, 2\pi]</m>. Clearly identify the <m>x</m>-intercepts.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    The <m>x</m>-intercepts are <m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(\pi, 0)</m>, and <m>(2\pi, 0)</m>.
                </p>
            </answer>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The sine function starts at 0, reaches its maximum of 1 at <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>, crosses the <m>x</m>-axis at <m>\pi</m>, reaches its minimum of -1 at <m>\frac{3\pi}{2}</m>, and completes its cycle at <m>2\pi</m> where it crosses the <m>x</m>-axis again.
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="fig-ex-sine-graph">
                    <caption>Graph of <m>y = \sin(x)</m></caption>
                    <image width="60%">
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2]
                                \draw[-&gt;] (-0.5,0) -- (6.8,0) node[right]{$x$};
                                \draw[-&gt;] (0,-1.5) -- (0,1.5) node[above]{$y$};
                                
                                \draw[domain=0:6.28,smooth,thick,blue] plot (\x,{sin(\x r)});
                                
                                \fill (0,0) circle (1.5pt) node[below left] {$0$};
                                \fill (3.14,0) circle (1.5pt) node[below left] {$\pi$};
                                \fill (6.28,0) circle (1.5pt) node[below left] {$2\pi$};
                                
                                \draw[dashed, gray] (1.57,0) -- (1.57,1) node[above, black] {$(\frac{\pi}{2}, 1)$};
                                \draw[dashed, gray] (4.71,0) -- (4.71,-1) node[below, black] {$(\frac{3\pi}{2}, -1)$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the domain restrictions (vertical asymptotes) for <m>y = \tan(x)</m> on the interval <m>[0, 2\pi]</m>. Explain why they occur.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>x = \frac{\pi}{2}</m> and <m>x = \frac{3\pi}{2}</m>. They occur because cosine is zero at these angles, making the tangent ratio undefined.
                </p>
            </answer>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Tangent is defined as <m>\tan(x) = \frac{\sin(x)}{\cos(x)}</m>. The function is undefined wherever the denominator, <m>\cos(x)</m>, equals zero. On the interval <m>[0, 2\pi]</m>, <m>\cos(x) = 0</m> at the top and bottom of the unit circle: <m>x = \frac{\pi}{2}</m> and <m>x = \frac{3\pi}{2}</m>. These create vertical asymptotes.
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="fig-ex-tan-asymptotes-1">
                    <caption>Graph of <m>y = \tan(x)</m> with vertical asymptotes</caption>
                    <image width="60%">
                        <shortdescription>A coordinate graph showing the periodic curves of y equals tangent of x from zero to two pi, bounded vertically between negative three and three with vertical dashed red asymptote lines at pi over two and three pi over two.</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
                                % Restrict the rendering area so elements do not bleed out
                                \clip (-0.8, -3.8) rectangle (7.5, 3.8);

                                % Draw Grid lines for y-axis references
                                \draw[gray!20, thin] (-0.5,-3) -- (6.5,-3);
                                \draw[gray!20, thin] (-0.5,-1.5) -- (6.5,-1.5);
                                \draw[gray!20, thin] (-0.5,1.5) -- (6.5,1.5);
                                \draw[gray!20, thin] (-0.5,3) -- (6.5,3);

                                % Axes
                                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-0.5,0) -- (6.8,0) node[right]{$x$};
                                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-3.3) -- (0,3.3) node[above]{$y$};
                                
                                % X-Axis Ticks
                                \foreach \x in {1.57, 3.14, 4.71, 6.28} {
                                    \draw[thick] (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1);
                                }
                                
                                % Axis Labels
                                \node[below left, font=\small] at (0,0) {$0$};
                                \node[below, font=\small, yshift=-2pt] at (3.14,0) {$\pi$};
                                \node[below, font=\small, yshift=-2pt] at (6.28,0) {$2\pi$};
                                
                                \node[left, font=\small] at (0,3) {$3$};
                                \node[left, font=\small] at (0,-3) {$-3$};

                                % Vertical Asymptotes 
                                \draw[dashed, thick, red!90!black] (1.5708, -3.5) -- (1.5708, 3.5);
                                \draw[dashed, thick, red!90!black] (4.7124, -3.5) -- (4.7124, 3.5);
                                
                                \node[above, red!90!black, font=\footnotesize] at (1.5708, 3.5) {$x=\frac{\pi}{2}$};
                                \node[above, red!90!black, font=\footnotesize] at (4.7124, 3.5) {$x=\frac{3\pi}{2}$};
                                
                                % Tangent curves scaled precisely to terminate near y = 3 and y = -3
                                \draw[domain=0:1.25,smooth,line width=1.2pt,teal] plot (\x,{tan(\x r)});
                                \draw[domain=1.89:4.39,smooth,line width=1.2pt,teal] plot (\x,{tan(\x r)});
                                \draw[domain=5.03:6.28,smooth,line width=1.2pt,teal] plot (\x,{tan(\x r)});
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>\sin(\theta) = \frac{3}{5}</m> and <m>\theta</m> is in Quadrant I, find the exact values of <m>\cos(\theta)</m> and <m>\tan(\theta)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>\cos(\theta) = \frac{4}{5}</m>, <m>\tan(\theta) = \frac{3}{4}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    We can model this situation using a right triangle in Quadrant I. The sine ratio is <m>\frac{\text{Opposite}}{\text{Hypotenuse}}</m>, so we can assign the opposite side a length of 3 and the hypotenuse a length of 5.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Using the Pythagorean theorem (<m>a^2 + b^2 = c^2</m>), we find the adjacent side:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>a^2 + 3^2 \amp = 5^2</mrow>
                        <mrow>a^2 + 9 \amp = 25</mrow>
                        <mrow>a^2 \amp = 16 \implies a = 4</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="fig-ex-right-triangle">
                    <caption>Right triangle for <m>\theta</m> in Quadrant I</caption>
                    <image width="40%">
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2]
                                \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (4,0) node[midway, below] {$4$} -- (4,3) node[midway, right] {$3$} -- (0,0) node[midway, above left] {$5$};
                                \draw (3.7,0) -- (3.7,0.3) -- (4,0.3);
                                \draw (0.6,0) arc (0:36.87:0.6);
                                \node at (0.9,0.3) {$\theta$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>

                <p>
                    Now we can find the remaining trigonometric ratios using the triangle:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p><m>\cos(\theta) = \frac{\text{Adjacent}}{\text{Hypotenuse}} = \frac{4}{5}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\tan(\theta) = \frac{\text{Opposite}}{\text{Adjacent}} = \frac{3}{4}</m></p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate the exact value of <m>\sin\left(\frac{7\pi}{6}\right)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>-\frac{1}{2}</m>
                </p>
            </answer>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The angle <m>\frac{7\pi}{6}</m> lies in Quadrant III because it is greater than <m>\pi</m> but less than <m>\frac{3\pi}{2}</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    To determine the exact value, first find the reference angle.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow> \frac{7\pi}{6}-\pi \amp = \frac{\pi}{6} </mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    The reference angle is therefore <m>\frac{\pi}{6}</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Since sine represents the vertical coordinate on the unit circle and all points in Quadrant III have negative <m>y</m>-coordinates, the sine value will be negative.
                </p>

                <p>
                    We know that
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow> \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right) \amp = \frac{1}{2} </mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    Hence,
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow> \sin\left(\frac{7\pi}{6}\right) \amp = -\frac{1}{2} </mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="fig-sine-seven-pi-six">
                    <caption>Evaluating sine at <m>\frac{7\pi}{6}</m></caption>

                    <image width="45%">
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2]
                                \draw (0,0) circle (1);
                                \draw[-&gt;] (-1.2,0)--(1.2,0);
                                \draw[-&gt;] (0,-1.2)--(0,1.2);

                                \coordinate (P) at ({cos(210)},{sin(210)});
                                \draw[thick,blue] (0,0)--(P);
                                \draw[dashed] ({cos(210)},0)--(P);

                                \filldraw[blue] (P) circle (1pt);

                                \node[below left] at (P)
                                {$\left(-\frac{\sqrt3}{2},-\frac12\right)$};

                                \draw[-&gt;] (0.3,0) arc (0:210:0.3);

                                \node at (-0.35,-0.2) {$\frac{7\pi}{6}$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </exercise>


        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A Ferris wheel rotates in a circular motion. A passenger starts at the middle height of the wheel and moves upward. The height of the passenger after time <m>t</m> seconds is modeled by
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow> h(t) \amp = 20+15\sin(t) </mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    Determine:
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The maximum height of the passenger.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The minimum height of the passenger.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The height of the passenger when <m>t=\frac{\pi}{2}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p><m>35</m></p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p><m>5</m></p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p><m>35</m></p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The function
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow> h(t) \amp = 20+15\sin(t) </mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    has a midline of <m>20</m> and an amplitude of <m>15</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Since the sine function ranges from <m>-1</m> to <m>1</m>, the largest possible value occurs when <m>\sin(t)=1</m>, while the smallest value occurs when <m>\sin(t)=-1</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Maximum height:
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>h_{\max} \amp = 20+15(1)</mrow>
                                    <mrow>\amp = 35</mrow>
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Minimum height:
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>h_{\min} \amp = 20+15(-1)</mrow>
                                    <mrow>\amp = 5</mrow>
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Substitute <m>t=\frac{\pi}{2}</m> into the model.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>h\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right) \amp = 20+15\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)</mrow>
                                    <mrow>\amp = 20+15(1)</mrow>
                                    <mrow>\amp = 35</mrow>
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                    </ol>
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="fig-ferris-wheel-sine">
                    <caption>Ferris wheel height modeled by a sine function</caption>

                    <image width="60%">
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
                                \draw[-&gt;] (-0.5,0)--(7,0) node[right] {$t$};
                                \draw[-&gt;] (0,0)--(0,4.5) node[above] {$h(t)$};

                                \draw[dashed] (0,2)--(6.5,2);
                                \node[left] at (0,2) {$20$};

                                \draw[domain=0:6.28,smooth,thick,blue]
                                plot (\x,{2+1.5*sin(\x r)});

                                \filldraw (1.57,3.5) circle (1.5pt);
                                \node[right] at (1.57,3.5) {$\left(\frac{\pi}{2},35\right)$};

                                \filldraw (4.71,0.5) circle (1.5pt);
                                \node[right] at (4.71,0.5) {$\left(\frac{3\pi}{2},5\right)$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

    </exercises>

    <!-- [LP] Add information on how to differentiate  -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>

        <table>
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                    <cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Provide a printed unit circle with the coordinates already labelled at each quadrantal angle, so learners can focus on reading sine and cosine values rather than constructing the diagram themselves. Limit the first few practice questions to angles at 0, 90, 180, and 270 degrees, where the coordinates come directly off the diagram. Pair struggling learners with a peer for the Ferris wheel exercise, since it combines several ideas at once.
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        <line>The core lesson activities as described above.</line>
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Ask learners to create their own real-world scenario that could be modelled by a sine or cosine function, similar to the Ferris wheel exercise, and to write the equation and two questions about it. Learners can also explore why the period of tangent is <m>\pi</m> while sine and cosine have a period of <m>2\pi</m>, using the unit circle to justify their reasoning.
                    </cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

    <exploration xml:id="explore-trigonometric-functions">
        <title>The unit circle and the trig graphs</title>
        <p>
            Move the angle slider to send the point around the unit circle. Its height gives <m>\sin\theta</m> and its horizontal distance gives <m>\cos\theta</m>. Matching points move along the sine, cosine and tangent graphs at the same time, so you can see how one angle fixes all three values.
        </p>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-trigonometric-functions" platform="jsxgraph" width="95%" aspect="2:1" source="jsxgraph/trigonometric-functions.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsx-trigonometric-functions" surface="jsxboard" aspect="2:1"/>
        </interactive>
    </exploration>

